<article class="env-proposition" id="0335">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0335">Proposition <span data-tag="0335">10.162.16</span>.</a> The following types of rings are Nagata and in particular universally Japanese: </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
fields, 
</p>
</li><li><p>
Noetherian complete local rings, 
</p>
</li><li><p>
$\mathbf{Z}$, 
</p>
</li><li><p>
Dedekind domains with fraction field of characteristic zero, 
</p>
</li><li><p>
finite type ring extensions of any of the above. 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      The Noetherian complete local ring case is Lemma <a href="/tag/032W" data-tag="032W">10.162.8</a>. In the other cases you just check if $R/\mathfrak p$ is N-2 for every prime ideal $\mathfrak p$ of the ring. This is clear whenever $R/\mathfrak p$ is a field, i.e., $\mathfrak p$ is maximal. Hence for the Dedekind ring case we only need to check it when $\mathfrak p = (0)$. But since we assume the fraction field has characteristic zero Lemma <a href="/tag/032M" data-tag="032M">10.161.11</a> kicks in. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>